Now that you know how binary works,
you need to learn how to convert a decimal number (like 13) into binary (1101).

The method: divide by 2 repeatedly, read remainders backward.

Step-by-step example: Convert 13 to binary

Step	|Division	|Result	|Remainder
------------------------------------
1	    |13 ÷ 2	  |6	    |1
2	    |6 ÷ 2	  |3	    |0
3	    |3 ÷ 2	  |1	    |1
4	    |1 ÷ 2	  |0	    |1
Now read the remainders from bottom to top: 1101

So 13 in decimal = 1101 in binary.

Another example: Convert 25 to binary

Step	|Division	|Result	|Remainder
----------------------------------
1	    |25 ÷ 2	  |12	    |1
2	    |12 ÷ 2	  |6	    |0
3	    |6 ÷ 2	  |3	    |0
4	    |3 ÷ 2	  |1	    |1
5	    |1 ÷ 2	  |0	    |1

Read bottom to top: 11001

So 25 in decimal = 11001 in binary.


WHY THIS WORKS:

Each time you divide by 2, you are finding the next binary digit
(starting from the rightmost). The remainder tells you whether that digit is 0 or 1.
You stop when the result becomes 0.
